        fp: "An avid numismatist entered a rare coin gallery to browse commemorative medallions. The gallery owner had recently purchased a box of assorted tarnished tokens from a general estate clearance for $200 with zero appraisal or provenance documentation. The owner sorted through the box, selected an unpolished, heavily oxidized silver coin bearing an obscure Latin inscription, and placed it in a display case with a card reading: 'Interesting antique Latin medallion — $300.' The numismatist, who had spent decades studying colonial-era Spanish mint marks, immediately recognized the medallion as an exceptionally rare, authenticated 1652 Peruvian silver royal presentation coin with an actual fair market value of $85,000. Without sharing this knowledge, the numismatist offered $300 cash. The gallery owner accepted the money and handed the coin over. Two weeks later, the gallery owner learned from an auction catalog that the numismatist had consigned the coin with an estimated hammer price of $90,000. The gallery owner sued the numismatist in equity to rescind the transaction based on unilateral mistake.",
